      title: "The Scale and Shape of the Trade",
      body: `## Counting the trade

For a long time, historians could only guess at how many Africans were carried to the Americas. In 1969 Philip Curtin, in *The Atlantic Slave Trade: A Census*, made the first systematic estimate from published sources, about 9.5 million arrivals. Since then, historians led by David Eltis and David Richardson have assembled records of some 36,000 slaving voyages into a database now available online as **Slave Voyages**. Their estimates are that between the early sixteenth century and the 1860s about **12.5 million** Africans were embarked on slave ships, and about **10.7 million** survived the voyage to the Americas. Nearly two million died at sea.

These numbers are hard to grasp. Until about 1820, more Africans than Europeans crossed the Atlantic to the Americas—several times as many. In demographic terms, the Americas before 1800 were as much an extension of Africa as of Europe.

## Where they went

The great majority of enslaved Africans went to **Brazil** and the **Caribbean**, to work on sugar plantations. Brazil received over 4.8 million; the British Caribbean about 2.3 million; the French Caribbean about 1.1 million; Spanish America over a million. Mainland North America received comparatively few: about **388,000** Africans landed directly in the territory that became the United States, under 4 percent of the total—though many more came via the Caribbean.

Yet by 1860 there were nearly four million enslaved people in the United States, more than in any other country in the Americas. The reason is one of the central facts of North American slavery: in the mainland colonies, especially in the Chesapeake, the enslaved population grew by **natural increase**, as births exceeded deaths. In the Caribbean and Brazil, by contrast, enslaved populations declined without continuous imports, because of brutal work regimes on sugar plantations, deadly tropical diseases, and sex ratios heavily weighted toward men. Jamaica imported over a million Africans; at emancipation in 1834, its enslaved population was about 310,000. The United States imported under 400,000; at emancipation in 1865, its enslaved population was about four million.

## Where they came from

Enslaved Africans came from a long stretch of coast, from Senegambia in the north to Angola in the south, and from the interior behind it. Historians identify several major regions of embarkation: **Senegambia**, the **Sierra Leone** and **Windward Coast**, the **Gold Coast** (modern Ghana), the **Bight of Benin** (the "Slave Coast," modern Togo, Benin, and western Nigeria), the **Bight of Biafra** (southeastern Nigeria), and **West-Central Africa** (Kongo and Angola), which supplied nearly half of all those carried across the Atlantic.

Different American regions drew from different African regions, at different times. Virginia received many people from the Bight of Biafra, mostly Igbo-speaking; South Carolina drew heavily from West-Central Africa and Senegambia; Louisiana, in its French period, from Senegambia. These patterns matter, because they shaped the languages, religions, and cultures that Africans brought with them.

## How captives were taken

Most captives were enslaved within Africa, by Africans: taken as prisoners of war, seized in raids and kidnappings, condemned for crimes or debts, or sold by their own rulers. Europeans rarely ventured inland; they bought captives at forts and trading posts on the coast, or from ships anchored offshore, in exchange for cloth (above all Indian cotton textiles), guns and gunpowder, iron and copper bars, alcohol, tobacco, and cowrie shells, which served as currency. The demand for captives encouraged wars and raids, and the guns that Europeans traded made them more destructive.

The journey from the interior to the coast could take months, and many died on the way. At the coast, captives were held in fortified **barracoons** or in the dungeons of forts such as Elmina and **Cape Coast Castle**, the English headquarters on the Gold Coast, sometimes for weeks or months, until a ship took them aboard.

## The North American trade

British North Americans participated in the slave trade not only as buyers but as carriers. Merchants in **Rhode Island**, especially Newport and Bristol, organized the majority of the slaving voyages launched from the mainland colonies, carrying rum distilled from West Indian molasses to Africa to exchange for captives. The so-called **triangular trade**—manufactured goods from Britain to Africa, captives from Africa to the Americas, plantation produce from the Americas to Britain—is a useful simplification, though many voyages did not follow such neat routes. The point is that the whole Atlantic economy, including the economies of New England and the Middle Colonies, which sold food, timber, and horses to the sugar islands, was bound up with the slave trade and the plantation system.

> **Pause and consider.** The United States imported far fewer Africans than Jamaica, yet ended with ten times as many enslaved people. What does this tell you about the differences between slave societies?`,
